#{[17592186235425 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186235426 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186235427 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{(x-1)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-3x+2}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\cdot sin⁡(2)$"] [17592186235428 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}\\frac{2f(x)}{(1+4x)^{3}}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}\\frac{2f(x)}{(1+4x)^{3}}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186235443 :question/problem "The broker predicts that, in the future, the stock will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$ Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186235443 :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/fe/fee4cee6127ed4c12bc3c68260a6c21917734a44b60273448e68dd832270b0e5.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186235446 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2{\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [17592186235450 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [17592186235468 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}"] [17592186235476 :question/problem "Write down an expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral, and express the integral as the limit of a left Riemann sum. You do not need to simplify your answers.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle A={\\int }_{4}^{7}\n$$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}"] [17592186235507 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{f(x)-{x}^{2}}{{f}^{′}(x)+2{e}^{x}}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ."] [17592186235508 :question/problem "The function $g$ is defined as\n$$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{-7}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\lim_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+6x-7}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ."] [17592186237784 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c309c0066e807d9a4bab21c3f1d6d2e967d1d502562f19a6e0f0a26d6c5118e.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [17592186237815 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7}}=\n$$"] [17592186237817 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}-3}=$"] [17592186237818 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1}=\n$$"] [17592186238203 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186238317 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})"] [17592186238725 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186238748 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186238752 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x)"] [17592186238755 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)>0"] [17592186239262 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186239476 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne 3"] [17592186239737 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)"] [17592186239975 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(3)"] [17592186240118 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186240120 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2)"] [17592186240178 :answer/value "$\\lim_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)$ does not exist"] [17592186240788 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$” is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement “$\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists” is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$” is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186240811 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5$ and $f(5)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)}\\,?\n$$"] [17592186240812 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=-6,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x}?$"] [17592186240813 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=13,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {7}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-f(x))?$"] [17592186240840 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-5$ and $\\lim_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=3,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}(f(x)-2f(-5))?$"] [17592186240841 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-9$ and $f(-9)=4,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-9)^{-}}(f(x)-x)^{2}?\n$$"] [17592186240842 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=-1$ and $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-6,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(2f(x)-xf(-1))?\n$$"] [17592186240855 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 0=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=-1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."] [17592186240856 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=1$” is false.  The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=2\\ne 4=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement  “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is true."] [17592186240864 :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)$ $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\n![](images/34/340443e20bc285d2e711b81608a60eb81ff33537ece23860b11871f5373dfde2.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1$ but they are *not* removable."] [17592186240868 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=-2,$ but it is not a point removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(-2)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-2$ is not a point removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."] [17592186240873 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=-2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=3$ is also a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally,  the function has a discontinuity at $x=5.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(5)$ does not exists $\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the first condition that failed, which tells us that $x=5$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=3$ only."] [17592186240875 :question/worked-solution "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=4.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(4)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)\\ne f(4)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=4$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=3$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=4$ only."] [17592186240900 :question/worked-solution "The function $y=g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $g(-3)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)=-2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](images/38/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png)\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-3,$ while $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities."] [17592186240910 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to ,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity."] [17592186240911 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."] [17592186241048 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡x}{5{x}^{2}}=\n$$"] [17592186243922 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5}=$"] [17592186243935 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}.$"] [17592186243935 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-2)^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$"] [17592186243936 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}.$"] [17592186243936 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 3.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}-2$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/9f/9fade66030e2e416f92138d787999d3ca3a7dd4788ad39cf29dd07ff944038da.png)"] [17592186243937 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}}.$"] [17592186243937 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{4}-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{4}-2x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{4}-2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/08/0865e078246952abcfcd570887a38edfb8120066cd6ee9ad54edc61bac5ddc52.png)"] [17592186243960 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}.$"] [17592186243960 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $2x-3$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ab/ab3f7928be50c25689a99751cd180e585d749bf0a5c253c8662f9b820bbf4bb7.png)"] [17592186243961 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x}.$"] [17592186243961 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-1}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 9.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $4x-1$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/64/64700491874195711ad7291ce0471561df307bd099b5b8a38c33269cf08f21ab.png)"] [17592186243962 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}.$"] [17592186243962 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{3}-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{3}-2x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{3}-2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/a9/a9d96a5a85181cddc6eb1bd5f79d6a0238ef16f060ddebf2dc4c48ef1911604f.png)"] [17592186244012 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-7}-1}{x-4}=\n$$"] [17592186244013 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{3x-1}-\\sqrt{8}}=\n$$"] [17592186247462 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]"] [17592186247465 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186247526 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186247536 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186247545 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186247598 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]"] [17592186247875 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{2i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186248278 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186248361 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] [17592186248658 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186248687 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186248762 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{9+\\frac{4k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186248830 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186249075 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{2i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186249122 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186249147 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]"] [17592186249155 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186249445 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] [17592186249614 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] [17592186249655 :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] [17592186250137 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$"] [17592186250137 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $b=2.$"] [17592186250207 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{-x}}=$"] [17592186250208 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$"] [17592186250209 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}=$"] [17592186250210 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$"] [17592186250211 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x+1}{({x}^{2}-4)^{2}-12x-21}=$"] [17592186250212 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$"] [17592186250423 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x}{x}.$"] [17592186250426 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x}{x+1}.$"] [17592186250429 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}.$"] [17592186250430 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}.$"] [17592186253449 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3}=\n$$"] [17592186253451 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{20nx}=\n$$"] [17592186253453 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nxy}=\n$$"] [17592186253455 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{\\pi n}=\n$$"] [17592186253460 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-6n}=\n$$"] [17592186253461 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n{y}^{2}}=\n$$"] [17592186259538 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n/2}}-1)=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n/2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n/2}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=-1+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n/2}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$"]}
